# config: mock-twoengine.json
#
# The same kibitz-while-reviewing workflow, but with the coach and the kibitz
# engine as *different processes* — which is what a real configuration looks
# like (GNU Go carrying "main", KataGo carrying "kibitz").
#
# It is a distinct code path, not a variation on the other one:
# NavCommand::KIBITZ_NAV short-circuits `kibitz == coach || coach->play(...)`,
# so a config where one engine carries both flags never asks the coach to accept
# the suggestion at all. Every other scenario uses such a config.
#
#   ./tests/run_scenarios.sh tests/scenarios/kibitz_two_engines.scn

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white coachmock
wait_idle

click 2 2
click 2 2
wait_until move_count >= 2
click 4 4
click 4 4
wait_until move_count >= 4

undo move
wait_idle
undo move
wait_idle
expect at_end false
expect phase paused

# The kibitz engine thinks for 2 s here, which is the point: a real one on a CPU
# backend takes far longer, and a nav-queue genmove used not to set
# `playerToMove` at all. `isThinking()` therefore stayed false for the whole
# search — the toolbar did not grey, no engine was named, navigation was not
# blocked, and a bug report described the silence as "nothing happened".
play once
wait 400
expect thinking true
expect can_navigate false
expect can_kibitz false

# And the wait is *visible*. Greying the toolbar was the whole of what the user
# got: nothing in the UI read isThinking(), so a genmove was the third silent
# wait in this program after engine loading and the post-resize resync, and by
# far the longest — 30.9 seconds measured for one kibitz from the stock 9x9
# KataGo on a CPU backend, which was duly reported as "nothing happens".
#
# Two things are needed and neither works alone: getIdleTimeout() has to keep
# waking the main loop, or no frame is drawn and no message can appear however
# well it is written — and there has to be a message. It names the engine for
# the same reason the loading line does; with two configured, "thinking" does
# not say which, and the one that matters is the slow one.
expect status_text kibitzmock is thinking…

wait_until move_count >= 3
expect at_end true
expect phase paused
expect thinking false
# It stands down again. A status line that stayed would be worse than none.
expect status_text ""
